    "text_blocks": [
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "To prevent ice dams on a Utah roof, stop warm indoor air from escaping into your attic and keep snow and meltwater moving off the roof edge. Heavy mountain snow and sunny winter days in Utah make freeze-thaw cycles common, so combining attic upgrades with winter roof care works best.",
        "snippet_highlighted_words": [
          "stop warm indoor air from escaping into your attic and keep snow and meltwater moving off the roof edge"
        ],
        "reference_indexes": [
          0,
          1,
          2,
          3,
          4
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Long-Term Prevention"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Seal air leaks: Close gaps around recessed lights, attic hatches, plumbing stacks, and chimneys so warm air does not reach the roof deck.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              2
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Upgrade attic insulation: Keep attic temperatures cold and even by maintaining proper ceiling insulation so snow on the upper roof does not melt prematurely.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              0,
              1,
              3
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Ensure balanced ventilation: Pair soffit intake vents with ridge or exhaust vents to keep the roof deck close to outdoor temperatures.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              0,
              1
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Add ice and water shield: When re-roofing or repairing eaves, install waterproof membrane along vulnerable edges and valleys to block water backup.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              5,
              6
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Winter Maintenance & Snow Control"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Rake snow after storms: Use a long-handled roof rake from the ground to pull snow off the lower 3 to 4 feet of the roof edge without damaging shingles.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              3,
              7
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Keep gutters and downspouts clear: Remove leaves and debris before winter, and check that downspouts stay open so melting snow can drain.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              0,
              1,
              3
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Avoid risky ice removal: Do not chip ice with hammers, axes, or sharp tools that tear shingles or injure you from a ladder. For stubborn spots, some guides suggest melting a drainage channel with calcium chloride inside a stocking laid across the dam, or hiring a professional steam/ice-removal service. For more Utah-specific context, see Utah Ice Damming: What You Need To Know.",
            "snippet_links": [
              {
                "text": "Utah Ice Damming: What You Need To Know",
                "link": "https://utahfloodcleanup.com/utah-ice-damming-what-you-need-to-know/"
              }
            ],
            "reference_indexes": [
              7,
              8,
              9
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "If you share a bit more about your setup, I can tailor this further:"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Your roof type (asphalt shingle, metal, or flat/low-slope) and attic access"
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Whether you are preventing future dams or dealing with active icicles or ceiling leaks right now"
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Your approximate elevation or city in Utah"
          }
        ]
      }
